browser-pipeline.bodyMarkdown:
This recipe-browser example uses the same architecture without giving away the event project:

```js
function renderRecipes(recipes) {
  recipeList.textContent = "";

  for (const recipe of recipes) {
    const item = document.createElement("li");
    item.textContent = recipe.name;
    recipeList.append(item);
  }
}

function recipesForMeal(mealType) {
  if (mealType === "All") {
    return allRecipes;
  }

  return allRecipes.filter((recipe) => recipe.meal === mealType);
}
```

Step by step:

1. `renderRecipes` owns DOM output.
2. `recipesForMeal` owns the selection rule.
3. The filter function returns data instead of manipulating the page.
4. The renderer receives data instead of deciding what category is active.

The Event Browser uses the same architecture with different data, names, markup, and output.
